\section*{10 Lectures and 42 Open Problems — Sum of Squares approximation ratio for Max-Cut}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that approximating Max-Cut above the Goemans--Williamson ratio is NP-hard on simple unweighted graphs; In manuscript 2 (family 102; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that for any fixed positive completeness and soundness errors, a deterministic polynomial reduction from 3SAT produces fixed-alphabet Unique Games with the requested gap.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), an affine game is composed directly with folded Gaussian sign tables. Excess stability reveals a hidden coordinate through restriction Fourier mass and vector-valued hashing, yielding endpoint labelers that contradict the outer game's low value.

For manuscript 2 (family 102; 2026-09-23), a latent quadratic alphabet and matrix-slice tests supply an outer permutation game. High acceptance decodes agreeing advice strategies, whereas clean-coordinate estimates from the unsatisfiable parity instance force their agreement to decay, giving the soundness contradiction.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
NP-hardness of beating the Goemans--Williamson ratio does not calculate the unconditional degree-four sum-of-squares gap. The separate near-bipartite rounding clause is also not provided, so this is supporting hardness information for a larger question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: approximating Max-Cut above the Goemans--Williamson ratio is NP-hard on simple unweighted graphs.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

For manuscript 2 (family 102; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: for any fixed positive completeness and soundness errors, a deterministic polynomial reduction from 3SAT produces fixed-alphabet Unique Games with the requested gap.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{DirectedFeedback.lean}, \texttt{MinUncut.lean}, \texttt{OptimalMaxCut.lean}, \texttt{UniqueGamesTheorem.lean}, \texttt{VertexCover.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.DirectedFeedback.main}, \texttt{OAI.MinUncut.main}, \texttt{OAI.OptimalMaxCut.main}, \texttt{OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.theorem11}, \texttt{OAI.VertexCover.every\_fixed\_factor\_below\_two}, \texttt{OAI.VertexCover.explicit\_cover\_gap}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

